<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-function-behavior">
  <title>Describing Function Behavior</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When we look at the graph of a function, we usually notice its overall shape before we notice any particular point.
      Where does the graph go up?
      Where does it go down?
      Where does it turn around?
      Which way does it bend?
    </p>
    <p>
      In this activity we introduce vocabulary that describes these features precisely.
      Much of calculus is about finding these features from a formula.
      For now, we practice reading them from graphs and building graphs that have them.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-behavior-describe" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Describing the shape of a graph</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The following definitions give us a common language for talking about how a graph behaves.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Increasing and Decreasing</title>
        <p>
          We say <q>a function <m>f(x)</m> is <term>increasing</term> on a set <m>S</m></q> to mean that for any two values <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> in <m>S</m>, if <m>b \gt a</m> then <m>f(b) \gt f(a)</m>.
          (That is, the function goes <em>up</em> as the independent variable goes to the right.)
        </p>
        <p>
          We say <q><m>f(x)</m> is <term>decreasing</term> on <m>S</m></q> to mean that for any two values <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> in <m>S</m>, if <m>b \gt a</m>, then <m>f(b) \lt f(a)</m>.
          (The function goes <em>down</em> as the independent variable goes to the right.)
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Local Extreme Values</title>
        <p>
          A function <m>f(x)</m> has a <term>local maximum</term> at <m>x=a</m> means that <m>f(a) \ge f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in some (usually small) interval that contains <m>a</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          It has a <term>local minimum</term> at <m>x=a</m> means that <m>f(a) \le f(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in some interval containing <m>a</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Concavity</title>
        <p>
          We say <q><m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave up</term> on a set <m>S</m></q> to mean the <term>slope</term> of <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on <m>S</m>.
          We say <q><m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave down</term> on <m>S</m></q> to mean the slope is decreasing on <m>S</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        <em>Note:</em> The slope will later be measured using the derivative in order to make the definition of concavity more precise.
        Visually, a function is concave up if the curve bends upward everywhere on the set.
        It is concave down if the curve bends downward on the set.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Inflection Points</title>
        <p>
          A function <m>f(x)</m> has an <term>inflection point</term> at <m>x=a</m> means that <m>f(x)</m> changes concavity at <m>x=a</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        On an interval where a graph is increasing or decreasing, and also concave up or concave down, it has one of four basic shapes.
        Each shape can be pictured as one quarter of a circle.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-basic-shapes">
        <caption>The four basic shapes of a graph</caption>
        <sidebyside widths="18% 18% 18% 18%">
          <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-shape-inc-cd">
            <caption>Increasing and concave down</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-behavior-shape-inc-cd">
              <shortdescription>
                The upper left quarter of a circle. Reading left to right, the curve rises steeply at first and then levels off.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm, y=2cm]
                  \path (-0.1,-0.1) rectangle (1.1,1.1);
                  \draw[very thick] (0,0) arc[start angle=180, end angle=90, radius=1];
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-shape-dec-cd">
            <caption>Decreasing and concave down</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-behavior-shape-dec-cd">
              <shortdescription>
                The upper right quarter of a circle. Reading left to right, the curve starts level and then falls more and more steeply.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm, y=2cm]
                  \path (-0.1,-0.1) rectangle (1.1,1.1);
                  \draw[very thick] (0,1) arc[start angle=90, end angle=0, radius=1];
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-shape-dec-cu">
            <caption>Decreasing and concave up</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-behavior-shape-dec-cu">
              <shortdescription>
                The lower left quarter of a circle. Reading left to right, the curve falls steeply at first and then levels off.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm, y=2cm]
                  \path (-0.1,-0.1) rectangle (1.1,1.1);
                  \draw[very thick] (0,1) arc[start angle=180, end angle=270, radius=1];
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-shape-inc-cu">
            <caption>Increasing and concave up</caption>
            <image xml:id="image-behavior-shape-inc-cu">
              <shortdescription>
                The lower right quarter of a circle. Reading left to right, the curve starts level and then rises more and more steeply.
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm, y=2cm]
                  \path (-0.1,-0.1) rectangle (1.1,1.1);
                  \draw[very thick] (0,0) arc[start angle=270, end angle=360, radius=1];
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>

      <p>
        For each graph below, describe the shape of the graph in complete sentences using this vocabulary.
        Your description should include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>the intervals where the function is increasing and the intervals where it is decreasing,</p></li>
        <li><p>the intervals where the function is concave up and the intervals where it is concave down,</p></li>
        <li><p>where the function has a local maximum or a local minimum, and</p></li>
        <li><p>where the function has an inflection point.</p></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A typical sentence might read <q><m>f</m> is decreasing and concave up on the interval <m>(a, b)</m>.</q>
        When a change in behavior does not happen at a whole number, estimate its location from the graph.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-describe-graph-1" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-graph-1">
          <caption>The graph of <m>y = f(x)</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="image-behavior-graph-1" width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
              A smooth curve shown for x between -6 and 8 and y between -5 and 15.
              It enters at the bottom left, rises to a peak at (-2, about 8.2), falls through (1, 4),
              reaches a low point at (4, about -0.2), and then rises steeply, leaving the top of the window near x = 7.8.
            </shortdescription>
            <!-- f(x) = ((x-1)^3 - 27(x-1))/13 + 4.
                 Local max at x = -2 (f = 106/13), local min at x = 4 (f = -2/13).
                 Inflection point at x = 1 (f = 4). Concave down on (-infty, 1), concave up on (1, infty). -->
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines = middle,
                  xlabel = {$x$}, ylabel = {$y$},
                  xmin = -6, xmax = 8,
                  ymin = -5, ymax = 15,
                  xtick = {-6,-5,...,8},
                  ytick = {-4,-2,...,14},
                  minor ytick = {-5,-3,...,15},
                  grid = both,
                  major grid style = {gray!30},
                  minor grid style = {gray!30},
                  tick label style = {font=\scriptsize},
                  axis line style = {->},
                  width = 8.5cm, height = 10cm,
                ]
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=-6:8, samples=200]
                    {((x-1)*(x-1)*(x-1) - 27*(x-1))/13 + 4};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Describe the shape of the graph of <m>f</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-describe-graph-2" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-graph-2">
          <caption>The graph of <m>y = g(x)</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="image-behavior-graph-2" width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
              A smooth W-shaped curve shown for x between -3 and 7 and y between 0 and 10.
              It enters at the top left, falls to a low point at (-1, 1), rises to a rounded peak at (2, about 5),
              falls to a second low point at (5, 1), and then rises, leaving the top of the window near x = 6.9.
            </shortdescription>
            <!-- g(x) = (x-5)^2 (x+1)^2 / 20 + 1.
                 Local min at x = -1 and x = 5 (g = 1), local max at x = 2 (g = 101/20 = 5.05).
                 Inflection points at x = 2 - sqrt(3) (about 0.27) and x = 2 + sqrt(3) (about 3.73).
                 Concave up on (-infty, 2-sqrt3) and (2+sqrt3, infty), concave down between. -->
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines = middle,
                  xlabel = {$x$}, ylabel = {$y$},
                  xmin = -3, xmax = 7,
                  ymin = 0, ymax = 10,
                  xtick = {-3,-2,...,7},
                  ytick = {1,2,...,10},
                  grid = major,
                  major grid style = {gray!30},
                  tick label style = {font=\scriptsize},
                  axis line style = {->},
                  width = 8.5cm, height = 8.5cm,
                ]
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=-3:7, samples=200]
                    {(x-5)*(x-5)*(x+1)*(x+1)/20 + 1};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Describe the shape of the graph of <m>g</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-describe-graph-3" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <figure xml:id="fig-behavior-graph-3">
          <caption>The graph of <m>y = h(x)</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="image-behavior-graph-3" width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
              A continuous curve shown for x between -4 and 8 and y between 0 and 8.
              It starts at (-4, 2) and bends upward as it rises to a sharp peak at (-1, 7).
              It then falls, bending upward, and levels off at (1, 4).
              It keeps falling, now bending downward, to a sharp low point at (3, 1).
              It rises, bending downward, and levels off at (5, 4).
              Finally it rises again, bending upward, to (8, 7).
            </shortdescription>
            <!-- h is built from pieces of parabolas:
                   on [-4,-1]: (5/9)(x+4)^2 + 2     increasing, concave up
                   on [-1, 1]: (3/4)(x-1)^2 + 4     decreasing, concave up
                   on [ 1, 3]: -(3/4)(x-1)^2 + 4    decreasing, concave down
                   on [ 3, 5]: -(3/4)(x-5)^2 + 4    increasing, concave down
                   on [ 5, 8]: (1/3)(x-5)^2 + 4     increasing, concave up
                 Local max at x = -1 and local min at x = 3 (both are sharp corners).
                 Inflection points at x = 1 and x = 5 (both have a horizontal tangent). -->
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines = middle,
                  xlabel = {$x$}, ylabel = {$y$},
                  xmin = -4, xmax = 8,
                  ymin = 0, ymax = 8,
                  xtick = {-4,-3,...,8},
                  ytick = {1,2,...,8},
                  grid = major,
                  major grid style = {gray!30},
                  tick label style = {font=\scriptsize},
                  axis line style = {->},
                  width = 8.5cm, height = 7cm,
                ]
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=-4:-1, samples=60] {(5/9)*(x+4)^2 + 2};
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=-1:1,  samples=60] {(3/4)*(x-1)^2 + 4};
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=1:3,   samples=60] {-(3/4)*(x-1)^2 + 4};
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=3:5,   samples=60] {-(3/4)*(x-5)^2 + 4};
                  \addplot[very thick, domain=5:8,   samples=60] {(1/3)*(x-5)^2 + 4};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Describe the shape of the graph of <m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-behavior-sketch" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Sketching a graph from its behavior</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now we reverse the process.
        Instead of reading behavior from a graph, we start with a description of the behavior and create a graph that has it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each description below is given by two number lines.
        The marked points on each number line are <term>break points</term>, where the behavior changes.
        The first number line shows where the function is increasing or decreasing.
        The second number line shows where the function is concave up or concave down.
        These charts look like the sign charts from our earlier activities.
        Later in the course, we will build charts like these using sign analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each description:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Sketch a graph of a function <m>f</m> that has all of the behavior shown on both number lines.</p></li>
        <li><p>Mark and label every local maximum, local minimum, and inflection point on your sketch.</p></li>
        <li><p>State the <m>x</m>-value where each one occurs.</p></li>
        <li><p>Compare your sketch with the rest of your group. Are the sketches identical? Which features must every correct sketch share?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-sketch-1" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <image xml:id="image-behavior-chart-1" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two number lines. The first has a break point at 2: increasing to the left of 2 and decreasing to the right of 2.
            The second has a break point at 0: concave up to the left of 0 and concave down to the right of 0.
          </shortdescription>
          <!-- Answer: local max at x = 2; inflection point at x = 0. -->
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm, y=1cm, font=\small]
              % Increasing and decreasing
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,1.5) {Increasing or\\decreasing};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,1.5) -- (8,1.5);
              \draw (2,1.65) -- (2,1.35) node[below] {$2$};
              \node[above] at (-3,1.6) {increasing};
              \node[above] at (5,1.6) {decreasing};
              % Concavity
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,0) {Concavity};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,0) -- (8,0);
              \draw (0,0.15) -- (0,-0.15) node[below] {$0$};
              \node[above] at (-4,0.1) {concave up};
              \node[above] at (4,0.1) {concave down};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-sketch-2" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <image xml:id="image-behavior-chart-2" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two number lines. The first has break points at -4, 0, and 4: decreasing to the left of -4,
            increasing from -4 to 0, decreasing from 0 to 4, and increasing to the right of 4.
            The second has break points at -4 and 4: concave up to the left of -4,
            concave down from -4 to 4, and concave up to the right of 4.
          </shortdescription>
          <!-- Answer: local min at x = -4 and x = 4, local max at x = 0;
               inflection points at x = -4 and x = 4.
               Note: this behavior forces sharp corners at x = -4 and x = 4. -->
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm, y=1cm, font=\small]
              % Increasing and decreasing
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,1.5) {Increasing or\\decreasing};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,1.5) -- (8,1.5);
              \draw (-4,1.65) -- (-4,1.35) node[below] {$-4$};
              \draw (0,1.65) -- (0,1.35) node[below] {$0$};
              \draw (4,1.65) -- (4,1.35) node[below] {$4$};
              \node[above] at (-6,1.6) {decreasing};
              \node[above] at (-2,1.6) {increasing};
              \node[above] at (2,1.6) {decreasing};
              \node[above] at (6,1.6) {increasing};
              % Concavity
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,0) {Concavity};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,0) -- (8,0);
              \draw (-4,0.15) -- (-4,-0.15) node[below] {$-4$};
              \draw (4,0.15) -- (4,-0.15) node[below] {$4$};
              \node[above] at (-6,0.1) {concave up};
              \node[above] at (0,0.1) {concave down};
              \node[above] at (6,0.1) {concave up};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-behavior-sketch-3" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <image xml:id="image-behavior-chart-3" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two number lines. The first has a break point at 0: decreasing to the left of 0 and increasing to the right of 0.
            The second has break points at -2 and 2: concave down to the left of -2,
            concave up from -2 to 2, and concave down to the right of 2.
          </shortdescription>
          <!-- Answer: local min at x = 0; inflection points at x = -2 and x = 2. -->
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm, y=1cm, font=\small]
              % Increasing and decreasing
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,1.5) {Increasing or\\decreasing};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,1.5) -- (8,1.5);
              \draw (0,1.65) -- (0,1.35) node[below] {$0$};
              \node[above] at (-4,1.6) {decreasing};
              \node[above] at (4,1.6) {increasing};
              % Concavity
              \node[anchor=east, align=right] at (-8.5,0) {Concavity};
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,0) -- (8,0);
              \draw (-2,0.15) -- (-2,-0.15) node[below] {$-2$};
              \draw (2,0.15) -- (2,-0.15) node[below] {$2$};
              \node[above] at (-5,0.1) {concave down};
              \node[above] at (0,0.1) {concave up};
              \node[above] at (5,0.1) {concave down};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
